WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.001 --> 00:00:02.502
- I'm sitting between, I think

00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:05.572
the two most employed people
in NFL media.

00:00:05.572 --> 00:00:07.674
Radio shows, TV shows—
- We're workin'.

00:00:07.674 --> 00:00:09.743
- But I mean, what's it like
for both of you guys?

00:00:09.743 --> 00:00:12.712
- Once you leave the game,
you realize

00:00:12.712 --> 00:00:14.080
"All right, I gotta bust my ass

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:16.249
and work just as hard
in my new craft."

00:00:16.249 --> 00:00:18.852
So, like, every opportunity
is like me being a rookie again

00:00:18.852 --> 00:00:20.120
and when I was a rookie

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:21.755
I walked in
to the Minnesota Vikings

00:00:21.755 --> 00:00:25.859
and I'm seein' Daunte Culpepper
in a Theismann fur

00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:28.695
and I'm seein' Randy Moss
swagged out with a baggy suit

00:00:28.695 --> 00:00:31.064
and I'm like,
"Damn, this is the NFL."

00:00:31.064 --> 00:00:33.066
These dudes pullin' up in
Bentleys with spinners on it.

00:00:33.066 --> 00:00:35.068
- Yeah.
- And I'm like—

00:00:35.068 --> 00:00:36.636
- In Minnesota.
- "I'm a baby. I'm a baby

00:00:36.636 --> 00:00:38.271
and I gotta work."

00:00:38.271 --> 00:00:40.140
Not only to make a name
for myself

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:42.075
but to, like,
get these guys' attention

00:00:42.075 --> 00:00:44.577
and that was my goal,
to get the greats' attention.

00:00:44.577 --> 00:00:47.580
So, like, what we do now
is if we gotta work one

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:50.550
two, three, four jobs, we want
to get attentions of the masses

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:51.651
cuz we're still workin'.

00:00:51.651 --> 00:00:52.986
- I do wanna jump off

00:00:52.986 --> 00:00:54.154
of something you said
a few minutes ago.

00:00:54.154 --> 00:00:55.789
- Oh, Randy Moss?
- Yeah.

00:00:55.789 --> 00:00:58.158
- I was gonna say, you
experienced young Randy Moss.

00:00:58.158 --> 00:01:00.126
- Right.
- You experienced old Randy Moss.

00:01:00.126 --> 00:01:01.428
- Right.
- The first time

00:01:01.428 --> 00:01:04.664
I met Randy Moss,
I knew immediately that

00:01:04.664 --> 00:01:08.468
if I got an "attaboy" pat
on the back from him

00:01:08.468 --> 00:01:10.904
I've arrived as a wide receiver.

00:01:10.904 --> 00:01:12.305
But I went about it
the wrong way

00:01:12.305 --> 00:01:13.973
because I was tryin'
to get his attention

00:01:13.973 --> 00:01:16.476
like a thirsty dude
at the end of the night.

00:01:16.476 --> 00:01:18.144
Like, I was like, "All right,
as soon as I hit him

00:01:18.144 --> 00:01:19.579
with this icebreaker,
we're gonna be

00:01:19.579 --> 00:01:21.347
best friends forever, man."
- [Laughing]

00:01:21.347 --> 00:01:24.150
And I remember one day, I was
getting ready for practice

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:26.319
and I think
it was Jermaine Dupri.

00:01:26.319 --> 00:01:29.255
He had a music video, and he's
wearin' the Marshall 88 jersey.

00:01:29.255 --> 00:01:31.458
So I'm like in my little
dorm room at training camp.

00:01:31.458 --> 00:01:33.293
I was like, "This is it, man."

00:01:33.293 --> 00:01:34.928
And I remember,
I was doing conditioning

00:01:34.928 --> 00:01:36.362
and I'm standing next to him,
and I'm literally

00:01:36.362 --> 00:01:38.131
like, gassin' myself
up like a dude

00:01:38.131 --> 00:01:40.467
about to walk across the bar
and ask a girl for their number.

00:01:40.467 --> 00:01:41.868
I was like, "All right, man.
Come on.

00:01:41.868 --> 00:01:43.503
Don't blow it, don't blow it."

00:01:43.503 --> 00:01:46.106
And I was like, "Hey, uh, Randy, um."
Voice was crackin'.

00:01:46.106 --> 00:01:47.707
- Yeah.
- I don't know what was goin' on.
I'm a grown man.

00:01:47.707 --> 00:01:50.643
And I was like, "Did you see
the Jermaine Dupri video?

00:01:50.643 --> 00:01:52.612
He had your jersey on, man."

00:01:52.612 --> 00:01:53.813
And he's runnin' next to me.

00:01:53.813 --> 00:01:55.281
He looks at me

00:01:55.281 --> 00:01:56.683
and he just keeps on runnin'.

00:01:56.683 --> 00:01:59.352
I was crushed!
- Ohhh!
- I was like, "Ugh."

00:01:59.352 --> 00:02:02.489
Listen, I wrote a girl a note,
"Do you like me, yes or no?"

00:02:02.489 --> 00:02:03.990
in the fifth grade,
and she crumpled it up

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:05.458
and threw it at my face.

00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:08.461
- Oh, man.
- Randy Moss hurt me more.

00:02:08.461 --> 00:02:10.163
Like, my feelings were crushed,
and I was like

00:02:10.163 --> 00:02:11.998
"Damn, I blew it."

00:02:11.998 --> 00:02:14.501
And then what's crazy is, like,
once I started makin' plays

00:02:14.501 --> 00:02:16.503
like, that's when he came in
and was like, "All right."

00:02:16.503 --> 00:02:17.904
Like, "I know you love the game

00:02:17.904 --> 00:02:19.472
and I know you're one
of the good ones."

00:02:19.472 --> 00:02:19.639
and I know you're one
of the good ones."

00:02:19.639 --> 00:02:21.207
- It's funny, I think,
to hear him say that

00:02:21.207 --> 00:02:22.709
because I don't think people
always realize

00:02:22.709 --> 00:02:24.677
that football players
can also be fanboys.

00:02:24.677 --> 00:02:26.713
First time Randy Moss
came on the plane

00:02:26.713 --> 00:02:30.049
I was Row 27 by the window,
and he said, "Can I sit here?"

00:02:30.049 --> 00:02:31.184
I was like [stuttering]

00:02:31.184 --> 00:02:32.752
"Yeah, sure, sure.

00:02:32.752 --> 00:02:35.155
You can sit here."
- Yeah, yeah.

00:02:35.155 --> 00:02:36.656
- And, I mean,
that's the way I felt

00:02:36.656 --> 00:02:38.658
but like Nate's sayin', too

00:02:38.658 --> 00:02:40.860
the one thing I learned from him
in a hurry

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:44.564
is his perspective
on the game was, like, amazing.

00:02:44.564 --> 00:02:46.699
He could almost see the game
almost like a quarterback.
- Yeah.

00:02:46.699 --> 00:02:50.203
- He understood offense
and defense, and, you know

00:02:50.203 --> 00:02:52.105
he understood
when this play was stupid

00:02:52.105 --> 00:02:53.873
and he wasn't gonna
tell the coach

00:02:53.873 --> 00:02:55.708
"That's a stupid play, Simms,"
you know, whatever it was.
- Yeah.

00:02:55.708 --> 00:02:58.344
- I mean, and I just, I really
enjoyed being around him.

00:02:58.344 --> 00:02:58.878
- I mean, and I just, I really
enjoyed being around him.

00:02:58.878 --> 00:03:01.014
- Calvin is the forgotten guy.
- Yeah.

00:03:01.014 --> 00:03:03.283
- If I'm creating a player
in "Madden"

00:03:03.283 --> 00:03:07.253
he's 6'5" running a 4.3,
and his name is Calvin Johnson.

00:03:07.253 --> 00:03:08.922
- Yeah.
- But he's never mentioned

00:03:08.922 --> 00:03:12.759
in the "Jerry Rice, T.O.,
Randy Moss" top three.

00:03:12.759 --> 00:03:14.727
But I would argue
he is physically as gifted

00:03:14.727 --> 00:03:16.196
as any of them, if not more.

00:03:16.196 --> 00:03:17.730
- No question.
I mean, it's the same individual

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:19.365
that broke Jerry Rice's
single-season record.
- Yeah.

00:03:19.365 --> 00:03:21.301
And I watched him do it.
- Right.

00:03:21.301 --> 00:03:24.270
- I watched games where
he would put up a buck 90

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:27.040
and we go to the meeting,
and after a loss

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:28.708
Shawn Jefferson,
who's a former player

00:03:28.708 --> 00:03:30.877
wide receiver—he was
our wide receivers coach

00:03:30.877 --> 00:03:33.046
He'd say, "Calvin, I need you to do more."
- [Laughing]

00:03:33.046 --> 00:03:36.082
And, you know, I'm thinkin'
like, "Damn, what you want?

00:03:36.082 --> 00:03:37.750
The dude go for 250?"
- Seriously.
- Yeah.

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:39.152
- And Calvin wouldn't
say anything.

00:03:39.152 --> 00:03:40.820
He was quiet.

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:43.089
You know, his mother, a doctor.
Dad, he worked on

00:03:43.089 --> 00:03:44.490
the railroad,
so he had this blend

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:46.759
of blue-collar and high IQ

00:03:46.759 --> 00:03:48.561
and that was his approach
to life

00:03:48.561 --> 00:03:51.164
and he would just nod his head
and say, "All right, Coach."

00:03:51.164 --> 00:03:52.665
Next game, go put up 300.

00:03:52.665 --> 00:03:55.935
Now, Randy was just pure speed
encapsulated.

00:03:55.935 --> 00:03:59.472
If they put shoulder pads
and cleats on Usain Bolt

00:03:59.472 --> 00:04:02.675
and he was actually a good
football player that can catch

00:04:02.675 --> 00:04:05.278
and track the ball like no
other, it would be Randy Moss.
- Yeah.

00:04:05.278 --> 00:04:07.113
- And Randy Moss had the greatest
late hands ever.

00:04:07.113 --> 00:04:09.849
Even though coaches say, "Don't
panic, remember your cues

00:04:09.849 --> 00:04:12.185
don't give anything away,"
like, we panic.

00:04:12.185 --> 00:04:14.020
Like, we don't remember our
cues, and we give everything away.

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:15.855
Like, when the ball's
in the air

00:04:15.855 --> 00:04:18.925
you start hearing your own
breath, and you go from—

00:04:18.925 --> 00:04:20.627
[Panting]

00:04:20.627 --> 00:04:21.995
And if you're
a really good DB

00:04:21.995 --> 00:04:23.296
I know it's a small detail

00:04:23.296 --> 00:04:24.764
but you can hear
a guy breathing hard

00:04:24.764 --> 00:04:27.700
and then you see his eyes
go from here to here—
- Yes.

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:29.502
- And that right there is a cue
that something's coming.

00:04:29.502 --> 00:04:31.271
- Right.
- Randy would, like, look

00:04:31.271 --> 00:04:34.374
and the ball would be comin',
and his face doesn't move

00:04:34.374 --> 00:04:36.142
his hands doesn't move,
his breath doesn't move

00:04:36.142 --> 00:04:38.211
and then right when it gets
to him, he's like—

00:04:38.211 --> 00:04:39.846
Velcro.

00:04:39.846 --> 00:04:42.615
Like, that's something
that you might not see ever.

00:04:42.615 --> 00:04:44.217
- No.
- I don't think so.

00:04:44.217 --> 00:04:46.019
- And that's not something you coach.
It's something that God said

00:04:46.019 --> 00:04:47.854
"Hey, Randy, I got
a little extra for you."

00:04:47.854 --> 00:04:50.623
- Right. You the man.
- That was awesome.

00:04:50.623 --> 00:04:51.958
- Nate Burleson's the man.
- He is the man.

